##  Chemotherapy-Induced Neutropenia / Febrile Neutropenia (CIN/FN)
##  QSP / PK-PD model
##  ============================================================================
##  Condition : Chemotherapy-induced neutropenia and its clinical consequence,
##              FEBRILE NEUTROPENIA.  The dose-limiting toxicity of essentially
##              every cytotoxic regimen, the commonest oncological emergency,
##              and the reason a large fraction of patients never receive the
##              dose intensity their trial protocol specified.
##
##  Authors   : Generated by Claude Code Routine (QSP Disease Model Library)
##  Engine    : mrgsolve (>= 1.5)   —   load with mrgsolve::mread()
##
##  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  THE ORGANISING IDEA
##  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  The absolute neutrophil count is not a state you can dose against, because
##  by the time it moves the event that set it has already happened.  The ANC
##  measured on day 10 was decided by the kill in the proliferating compartment
##  on days 0-2, and everything in between is a DELAY LINE:
##
##      PROL -> TR1 -> TR2 -> TR3(storage) -> CIRC        (Friberg 2002)
##
##      the DELAY (mean transit time MTT)      sets WHEN the nadir arrives
##      the EXPOSURE (slope * Cp)              sets HOW DEEP it goes
##      the FEEDBACK exponent gamma            sets HOW FAST it comes back
##
##  These three are structurally separate parameters.  Nearly every clinical
##  rule about myelosuppression is a consequence of that separation rather than
##  of potency, and the model is arranged so that each rule can be derived
##  rather than asserted:
##
##    * G-CSF cannot raise a nadir that is already committed.  It shortens the
##      delay, slows the clearance of the cells that survive, and empties the
##      storage pool.  That is why its TIMING (24-72 h after chemotherapy, not
##      day 8) determines its effect and its DOSE barely does.
##    * Febrile-neutropenia risk is not a function of nadir depth but of the
##      INTEGRAL of depth over time below the 0.5 x 10^9/L threshold -- so two
##      patients with identical nadirs can carry very different risk.
##    * Everything that raises the measured ANC is not the same as everything
##      that restores host defence.  Corticosteroid demargination, storage-pool
##      release and prolonged circulating survival all raise the NUMBER without
##      adding marrow OUTPUT.  Neutrophil FUNCTION is therefore a separate
##      state, and the model's effective defence is COUNT x FUNCTION.
##    * The clinical decision is never "how low will it go".  It is G-CSF
##      (which preserves dose intensity) versus dose reduction (which does not),
##      so the model carries a tumour compartment purely to price that trade.
##
##  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  SCOPE — 53 ODE compartments
##  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  (a) 15 drug states
##      A_C/A_P1/A_P2      cytotoxic slot A, 3-compartment (taxane, topotecan)
##      B_C/B_P1           cytotoxic slot B, 2-compartment (anthracycline, gem)
##      C_C/C_P1           cytotoxic slot C, 2-compartment (alkylator, platinum)
##      D_C/D_P1           cytotoxic slot D, 2-compartment (etoposide)
##      G_DEP/G_C/G_P/G_E  G-CSF SC depot, central, peripheral, EFFECT site
##      T_C/T_P            trilaciclib
##      DEX                dexamethasone           ABX  levofloxacin
##  (b) 20 haematopoietic states
##      HSC                        quiescent stem pool = marrow reserve
##      PROL/TR1/TR2/TR3/CIRC/MARG neutrophil chain + storage + marginated
##      PROLP/TP1/TP2/TP3/PLT      megakaryocyte chain + platelets
##      PROLE/TE1/TE2/TE3/HB       erythroid chain + haemoglobin
##      TPO                        thrombopoietin (c-Mpl-mediated clearance)
##      FUNC                       neutrophil functional capacity
##      FIB                        irreversible marrow damage
##  (c) 6 host-defence and inflammation states
##      BAR   mucosal barrier integrity      BACT  translocated bacterial load
##      IL6   CRP   TDEV (temperature deviation from 36.8 C)
##  (d) 10 counters and outcome states
##      AUC_A/AUC_B/AUC_C/AUC_D  cytotoxic exposure
##      AUC_G/AUC_T              G-CSF and trilaciclib exposure
##      DSN_H                    hours spent with ANC < 0.5 (the real endpoint)
##      CUMHAZ                   cumulative febrile-neutropenia hazard
##      TUMS/TUMR                drug-sensitive and resistant tumour clones
##
##  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  UNITS
##  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  time            hours; t = 0 is the first chemotherapy administration
##  cytotoxics      umol (amounts), umol/L (concentrations)
##  G-CSF           ng (amounts), ng/mL (concentrations)
##  trilaciclib     mg, mg/L         dexamethasone mg      levofloxacin mg
##  cell counts     10^9/L "virtual concentration", the Friberg convention
##  haemoglobin     g/dL             bacteria  CFU-equivalent (internal scale)
##
##  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  CALIBRATION — two fitted parameters, and a trade-off that decided the rest
##  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  Structural parameters are FIXED at their published values and never fitted:
##    MTT = 89.3 h and gamma = 0.170                     Friberg 2002 (12488418)
##    circulating neutrophil t1/2 = 7.9 h                Dancey 1976 (956397)
##    marginated pool = circulating pool                 Athens 1961 (13684958)
##    docetaxel CL 21.6 L/h/m2, V1 7.4 L/m2              Bruno 1998 (9440742)
##    carboplatin CL(mL/min) = GFR + 25                  Calvert 1989 (2681557)
##    pegfilgrastim: renal clearance ~ 0, cleared by     Roskos 2006 (16809800)
##      receptor-mediated internalisation only           Layton 1989 (2475185)
##
##  FITTED, one parameter per reported number:
##    HFN_BASE  <- febrile neutropenia 1% on pegfilgrastim   Vogel 2005 (15718314)
##    HFN_K     <- febrile neutropenia 17% on placebo        Vogel 2005
##
##  ONLY TWO, and the reason is a trade-off the model cannot escape.  The overall
##  myelotoxic scale SENS_GLOBAL can be set EITHER so that the absolute durations
##  of severe neutropenia reported in the G-CSF trials come out right, OR so that
##  the model tells regimens apart -- not both.  The duration below the 0.5
##  threshold SATURATES in this topology: the recovery limb starts from a floor
##  set by PHI, which does not depend on exposure, and climbs at a rate set by
##  the feedback exponent, so once the proliferative pool is empty at all, extra
##  exposure buys almost no extra duration.  Raising the scale until CAE reaches
##  Crawford 1991's 6-day median puts EVERY combination regimen at a nadir of
##  0.065-0.11 and a DSN of 5.4-6.8 days, i.e. it stops telling AC apart from
##  TAC.  SENS_GLOBAL = 2.0 is therefore chosen for DISCRIMINATION, and the
##  resulting shortfall against the absolute durations is REPORTED rather than
##  tuned away.  Analysis A1 of cin_reference_impl.py lists six such misses:
##
##    CAE without G-CSF, median DSN        6.0 d observed   model -23%
##    CAE + filgrastim d4-17, median DSN   1.0 d observed   model +90%
##    AT + pegfilgrastim, cycle-1 DSN      1.8 d observed   model -100%
##    AT + daily filgrastim, cycle-1 DSN   1.6 d observed   model  -9%
##    intact marrow + filgrastim, ANC peak 4-6 fold         model -62%
##    AC q3w P(FN) vs its guideline band   0.10-0.20        model at the top
##
##  Read together they say something specific: the model exaggerates how much
##  the TIMING of a growth factor matters, and it ranks pegfilgrastim above
##  daily filgrastim where Green 2003 found the opposite.  What survives is
##  everything COMPARATIVE -- change one thing at a time within a fixed regimen
##  and the comparison does not depend on the absolute scale being right.
##
##  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  WHAT THE MODEL IS NOT
##  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
##  * The nadir DAY is a function of MTT alone, so the model cannot reproduce
##    an agent-specific nadir day (carboplatin's is reported near day 21).
##  * The FN hazard is calibrated to a randomised-trial FN rate; trial rates
##    understate real-world rates (Truong 2016, 26712901), so every absolute
##    P(FN) here is a trial-population number.
##  * The tumour layer is a deliberately thin log-kill.  Its only job is to put
##    a price on dose reduction and cycle delay.  It is not a survival model.
##
##  Disclaimer: research / education / hypothesis generation only.  Not for
##              clinical decisions, prescribing, or regulatory submission.
##  ============================================================================

[PROB]
QSP model of chemotherapy-induced neutropenia posed as a DELAY problem rather
than a potency problem: cytotoxic exposure kills cycling granulocytic
progenitors on days 0-2, a four-stage maturation chain of fixed mean transit
time then determines when the resulting deficit reaches the blood, a weak
power-law feedback determines how fast it recovers, and febrile neutropenia
follows from the INTEGRAL of the deficit against a mucosal barrier the same
drug has just breached.  G-CSF is resolved into five distinct actions, only two
of which touch marrow output; its own clearance is mediated by the neutrophils
it creates, which makes a single fixed dose self-titrating.  Trilaciclib acts
by removing progenitors from the drug-sensitive cycling fraction.  The platelet
and erythroid lineages share the stem pool but run on their own clocks, and a
tumour compartment prices the dose reduction that neutropenia provokes.

[PLUGIN] Rcpp

